## Approach
Treat the grid as a graph where each `'1'` is a node connected to its four orthogonal neighbors. Scan every cell; when I hit an unvisited `'1'`, I run a BFS from it, marking cells as `'0'` as I enqueue them so nothing gets counted twice. Each BFS launch is one island.

## Code
```go
func numIslands(grid [][]byte) int {
	if len(grid) == 0 || len(grid[0]) == 0 {
		return 0
	}
	rows, cols := len(grid), len(grid[0])
	count := 0
	dirs := [4][2]int{{1, 0}, {-1, 0}, {0, 1}, {0, -1}}

	for r := 0; r < rows; r++ {
		for c := 0; c < cols; c++ {
			if grid[r][c] != '1' {
				continue
			}
			count++
			grid[r][c] = '0'
			queue := [][2]int{{r, c}}
			for len(queue) > 0 {
				cur := queue[0]
				queue = queue[1:]
				for _, d := range dirs {
					nr, nc := cur[0]+d[0], cur[1]+d[1]
					if nr < 0 || nr >= rows || nc < 0 || nc >= cols || grid[nr][nc] != '1' {
						continue
					}
					grid[nr][nc] = '0'
					queue = append(queue, [2]int{nr, nc})
				}
			}
		}
	}
	return count
}
```

## Complexity
Time is O(rows × cols) since each cell is enqueued at most once. Space is O(rows × cols) for the queue in the worst case, a single large island.

